questions.a:
```css
form.login-form input[type="text"] {
  border: 2px solid blue;
  padding: 10px;
}
```
A wrong answer (e.g., missing the attribute selector, using the wrong combinator, or omitting the class on the form) shows the student cannot combine class, descendant, and attribute selectors — a skill needed for real-world form styling.

```css
.tooltip {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
}
.tooltip::after {
  content: attr(data-tip);
  position: absolute;
  bottom: 100%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translateX(-50%);
  background: #333;
  color: white;
  padding: 4px 8px;
  border-radius: 4px;
  white-space: nowrap;
  opacity: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  transition: opacity 0.2s;
}
.tooltip:hover::after {
  opacity: 1;
}
```
A poor answer (e.g., missing `position: relative` on the parent, using `display: none` instead of opacity, or forgetting `content`) reveals the student cannot synthesize positioning, pseudo-elements, and hover state — a hallmark of moving from copying CSS to engineering it.
